WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.680
An earthquake is a sudden release of energy stored in rock.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.300
Seismic waves carry part of that energy outward through Earth and

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.520
along its surface; the disturbance can be detected far from its

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.200
source. The hypocenter, also called the focus, is the place where

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.140
rupture begins. The epicenter is the point on the surface directly

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.420
above it. A rupture can spread far beyond its starting point.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.840
A seismic station contains instruments that record ground motion.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.860
For comparable paths, a nearby station receives a given wave before

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.680
a more distant station. Read the actual station positions rather than

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.020
memorizing a station number.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.200
A normal fault lowers the hanging wall relative to the footwall

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.600
and commonly accommodates extension. A reverse fault raises the hanging wall;

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.680
a gently dipping reverse fault is a thrust fault.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.340
A strike-slip fault displaces its two sides mainly horizontally.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.800
Normal, reverse, thrust, and strike-slip faults can all generate earthquakes when

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.800
they slip abruptly. The fault type describes relative movement.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.780
It does not, by itself, supply the earthquake magnitude, hypocentral depth,

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:01.900
or expected damage.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.460
Explosive eruptions, magma pushing into rock, faulting beneath a heavy volcano,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.740
and collapse of unstable volcanic slopes can produce seismic signals.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.240
Distinctive volcanic tremors can accompany magma movement.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.060
Large landslides shake the ground as rock breaks and moves.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:24.040
Underground explosions compress surrounding material and generate waves that seismic networks

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.200
can detect. Reservoir filling adds a load to the crust;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.000
deep wastewater injection can alter conditions on existing faults.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.240
These are human-induced mechanisms, not evidence that every earthquake near a

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.620
well or reservoir has the same cause.
